<sn><md>### Changing a Variable's Value

A variable can be assigned a new value later:

```c
int age = 20;

age = 25;
```

The value of `age` is now `25`.

```text
Initially:  age → 20
After assignment: age → 25
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Assignment Using Expressions

You can also assign the result of an expression:

```c
int a = 10;
int b = 20;

int sum;

sum = a + b;
```

The computer calculates:

```text
sum = 10 + 20
sum = 30
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Initialization vs Assignment

| Initialization                                 | Assignment                                 |
| ---------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| Gives the first value during variable creation | Gives or changes a value after declaration |
| `int age = 20;`                                | `age = 20;`                                |
| Declaration and initialization happen together | The variable already exists                |
</md></sn><sn><md>### Complete Example

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int age;       // Declaration

    age = 20;      // Assignment

    age = 25;      // Reassignment

    printf("%d", age);

    return 0;
}
```

**Output:**

```text
25
```

&gt; **In short:** Variable assignment is the process of storing or updating a value in an already declared variable using the assignment operator (`=`).
</stdio.h></md></sn><sn><md>### Example

```c
int age;       // Declaration
age = 20;      // First Assignment or Initialization

int number = 10; // Declaration + Initialization
number = 15;     // Assignment

```</md></sn>